= Installing Oracle 10g on Fedora Core 2 =

== Pretend to be Red Hat Enterprise Linux ES Release 3 ==

Oracle 10g is only officially supported on a few versions of Redhat Linux and UnitedLinux. The installer checks /etc/redhat-release to see exactly what version of Redhat Linux you are running. To pretend that you are running Red Hat Enterprise Linux ES Release 3, run the following commands (as root):

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
mv /etc/redhat-release /etc/redhat-release.old
echo "Red Hat Enterprise Linux ES release 3 (Taroon Update 1)" > /etc/redhat-release
}}}
}}}

== Configure the Kernel ==

Oracle 10g requires that you at least temporarily increase the values of a couple of kernel parameters. To temporarily increase these values, follow these instructions (as root):

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
echo 65536 > /proc/sys/fs/file-max
echo 2147483648 > /proc/sys/kernel/shmmax
echo "250 32000 100 128" > /proc/sys/kernel/sem
}}}
}}}

== Install Oracle ==

Now, log in as the oracle user.

Since Oracle 10.1.0 uses a graphical installer, you need to be running X Windows or point the DISPLAY environment variable to an X Windows session running somewhere.

I have had problems with the graphical installer on enlightenment and fluxbox window managers in the past, though they may work for you.

If you have a CD, mount it and cd to the mount point. If you have a cpio distribution, extract the file using:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
gunzip ship.db.cpio
cpio -idmv < ship.db.cpio
}}}
}}}

Change directories to the Disk1 directory.[[br]][[br]] Type '''./runInstaller'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Welcome''' screen click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Inventory directory and credentials''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''OK'''[[br]][[br]] A dialog will pop up and prompt you to run /u01/app/oracle/oraInventory/orainstRoot.sh as root.[[br]]Follow the instructions and click '''Continue'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify File Locations''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Select Installation Type''' screen, accept the default selection '''Enterprise Edition''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] The installer will run a series of checks, if you configured the kernel properly before beginning the install, all should succeed.[[br]][[br]] On the '''Select Database Configuration''' screen, accept the default selections '''Create a starter database''' and '''General Purpose''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Database Configuration Options''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Database Management Option''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Database File Storage Option''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Backup and Recovery Options''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Specify Database Schema Passwords''' screen, enter and confirm passwords for the '''SYS, SYSTEM, SYSMAN''' and '''DBSNMP''' users. Note that you cannot use the classic "manager" and "change_on_install" passwords. Then click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Summary''' screen, click '''Install'''[[br]][[br]] The '''Install''' screen will show the progress of the installation[[br]][[br]] When installation is finished, the '''Configuration Assistants''' screen will show the progress of the initial configuration processes.[[br]][[br]] A '''Database Configuration Assistant''' dialog should pop up, displaying the progress of the initial database configuration. This is likely to fail. If it does, execute these command in another terminal window:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
cd $ORACLE_HOME/bin
mv oracle oracle.bin
cat > oracle <<"EOF"
#!/bin/bash
 
export DISABLE_HUGETLBFS=1
exec $ORACLE_HOME/bin/oracle.bin $@
EOF
chmod +x oracle
}}}
}}}

Then click Retry. During the '''Completing Database Creation''' phase, two dialogs may pop up with the following error: ORA-00988: missing or invalid password(s). These //appear// to be harmless. For each, just click '''OK'''.[[br]][[br]] When database creation is complete, a dialog will pop up with a summary. Click '''OK'''.

A dialog will pop up asking you to run root.sh. Just follow the directions on the dialog (they may take a while to complete) and click '''Ok'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''End Of Installation''' screen click '''Exit'''[[br]][[br]] A dialog will pop up asking: Do you really want to exit? Click '''Yes'''.

== Post-Installation ==

Sometimes, for some reason, during an installation, the tnslistener does not get configured to listen for connections to the ora1 database. Take a look at /u01/app/oracle/product/10.1.0/network/admin/listener.ora and make sure that there is an entry like the following:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
SID_LIST_LISTENER =
  (SID_LIST =
    (SID_DESC =
      (SID_NAME = PLSExtProc)
      (ORACLE_HOME = /u01/app/oracle/product/10.1.0)
      (PROGRAM = extproc)
    )
    (SID_DESC =
      (SID_NAME = ora1)
      (ORACLE_HOME = /u01/app/oracle/product/10.1.0)
    )
  )
}}}
}}}

The second SID_DESC section may be missing from your listener.ora file. If it is, update your listener.ora file, adding the necessary section.

During the installation, several ancillary processes were started. You can shut some of them down by executing the following commands (as oracle):

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
isqlplusctl stop
emctl stop dbconsole
emctl stop agent
}}}
}}}

and the following command as root:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
/etc/rc.d/init.d/init.cssd stop
}}}
}}}

At this point, only the database and tns listener should be running.

Unlike previous versions of oracle, Oracle 10g installs the init script /etc/rc.d/init.d/init.cssd. It is symlinked to the following locations:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
/etc/rc.d/rc5.d/S96init.cssd
/etc/rc.d/rc5.d/K96init.cssd
/etc/rc.d/rc3.d/S96init.cssd
/etc/rc.d/rc3.d/K96init.cssd
}}}
}}}

If you don't want the cssd to start at boot time, you can disable it by removing the following files:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
/etc/rc.d/rc5.d/S96init.cssd
/etc/rc.d/rc3.d/S96init.cssd
}}}
}}}

